# Packaging termusic

Everything here exists so a distribution packager does not have to reverse
engineer the project. termusic is a **client**: the MPD daemon is an external
backend and is never installed, started, stopped or configured by termusic.

## Metadata

| field | value |
|---|---|
| binary | `termusic` (installed to `${prefix}/bin/termusic`) |
| version | from `project(termusic VERSION ...)` in `CMakeLists.txt`; `termusic --version` prints `termusic <version>` |
| license | `GPL-3.0-or-later` (see `LICENSE`) |
| architecture | any the toolchain supports; release archives are built for `x86_64` |
| build system | CMake ≥ 3.20 + Ninja or Make, a C++20 compiler |

## Runtime dependencies

| dependency | kind | why |
|---|---|---|
| `libmpdclient.so.2` | **hard**, dynamic | the MPD protocol client |
| `libfftw3f.so.3` | **hard**, dynamic | the audio spectrum analyzer behind the visualizer |
| `libstdc++`, `libm`, `libc` | hard, dynamic | the C++ runtime |
| FTXUI | **static**, linked in | the terminal UI (no runtime package needed) |
| `mpd` (the daemon) | **optional / recommended** | only for users who want a *local* backend; a remote MPD works with no local daemon at all |

License obligations are recorded in `THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md`, which ships in the
archive and in `${prefix}/share/doc/termusic/`. Short version: FTXUI (MIT) is
linked **statically**, so its notice must accompany the binary; libmpdclient
(`BSD-2-Clause OR BSD-3-Clause`) and FFTW 3 (`GPL-2.0-or-later AND MIT AND
BSD-2-Clause`) are **dynamic** dependencies whose licenses are shipped by their
own distribution packages. termusic itself is `GPL-3.0-or-later`, which is
compatible with FFTW's "or later" terms.

Verify the exact list for a built binary with `ldd path/to/termusic`.

Consequences for package metadata:

* `Requires:` / `Depends:` must list `libmpdclient` and `fftw3f` (plus the C++
  runtime), never `mpd`.
* `Recommends:` (deb) / `Suggests:` (rpm) is the right place for `mpd`, together
  with a note that termusic connects to `127.0.0.1:6600` by default.
* Do **not** ship an MPD service unit, do not enable `mpd.service`, and do not
  add any scriptlet that starts MPD. termusic never manages the daemon.

## Install

```bash
cmake -S . -B build/release -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr
cmake --build build/release
DESTDIR="$pkgdir" cmake --install build/release
```

That installs:

```
${prefix}/bin/termusic
${prefix}/include/termusic/plugin_api.h
${prefix}/share/doc/termusic/README.md
${prefix}/share/doc/termusic/LICENSE
${prefix}/share/doc/termusic/THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md
${prefix}/share/doc/termusic/config.example.toml
${prefix}/share/doc/termusic/README.packaging
```

No tests, fixtures, captures or build files are installed. Runtime paths are
resolved through the XDG base directories (`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/termusic`,
`$XDG_DATA_HOME/termusic`, `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/termusic`), so nothing depends on the
source tree or the working directory.

## What has actually been built

For **v0.1.0** the published artifact is the tar.gz only. The packaging metadata
in this directory is prepared and, where the tooling exists, verified:

| target | state on the machine this was prepared on |
|---|---|
| runtime archive | **BUILT** -- `./build/release.sh`, see below |
| RPM | **BUILT, not published** -- `rpmbuild -ba packaging/termusic.spec` into a throwaway `_topdir` under `build/dist/rpmbuild/` produced `termusic-0.1.0-1.fc44.x86_64.rpm` (plus the source RPM). No `sudo`, nothing installed on the host. |
| DEB | **METADATA ONLY** -- the machine has no `dpkg-buildpackage`, `dpkg-source` or `debhelper`, so `debian/` has been written and reviewed but never executed. |

Nothing here claims a distribution repository, a signed package, or a package
built on a distribution that was not used. The RPM's `Recommends: mpd` is the
only place a local daemon is suggested, and it is deliberately weak: a remote
MPD needs no local one.

## Release archive

```bash
./build/release.sh          # -> build/dist/termusic-<version>-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
```

The script builds the release binary, stages exactly the files below and writes
a `.sha256` next to the archive:

```
termusic-<version>-linux-x86_64/
├── termusic
├── README.md
├── LICENSE
├── THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md
└── config.example.toml
```
